<header class="ref-head yr-head"><p class="cty-kicker">Year by year · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p><h1 class="research-title">1962</h1><p class="ref-lede">1962 in world history: 33 events, 5 births, 20 deaths, 2 works, 8 states founded and 10 that came to an end. 325 recorded states existed that year.</p></header>
<nav class="ref-letters yr-near" aria-label="Nearby years"><a href="/years/1958">1958</a><a href="/years/1959">1959</a><a href="/years/1960">1960</a><a href="/years/1961">1961</a><a href="/years/1962" aria-current="page">1962</a><a href="/years/1963">1963</a><a href="/years/1964">1964</a><a href="/years/1965">1965</a><a href="/years/1966">1966</a></nav>
<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-7">7 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">US embargo on trade with Cuba takes effect</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">A proclamation by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-f-kennedy">John F. Kennedy</a> banning almost all imports from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> came into force. Extended since, it is one of the longest-lasting trade embargoes in modern history.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-10">10 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">U-2 pilot Gary Powers exchanged for Soviet spy Rudolf Abel</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">On the Glienicke Bridge between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-berlin">West Berlin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potsdam">Potsdam</a>, the US pilot shot down in 1960 was swapped for the KGB colonel Rudolf Abel. The bridge became known for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> exchanges.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/february-20">20 February</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">John Glenn becomes the first American to orbit Earth</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Aboard Friendship 7, Glenn circled the Earth three times in under five hours. Ten months after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-gagarin">Yuri Gagarin</a>'s flight, it restored American confidence in the space race.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/algiers" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/algiers.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-5">5 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Algeria becomes independent from France</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a> proclaimed independence, 132 years to the day after French troops took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>. It followed an eight-year war of liberation and a referendum in which Algerians voted overwhelmingly for independence.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-10">10 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Telstar is launched, the first satellite to relay live TV</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">NASA launched AT&amp;T's Telstar 1 from Cape Canaveral. Within hours it relayed the first television pictures across the Atlantic, opening the age of satellite communications.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-5">5 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Marilyn Monroe is found dead in Los Angeles</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Marilyn Monroe was found dead at her home in Brentwood, aged 36; the coroner ruled probable suicide from barbiturates. Her death cut short the career of Hollywood's most famous star.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-31">31 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Trinidad and Tobago becomes independent</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad-and-tobago">Trinidad and Tobago</a> gained independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-williams">Eric Williams</a> as prime minister. Oil and gas wealth made it one of the most prosperous nations in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-12">12 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Kennedy tells Rice University 'we choose to go to the Moon'</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">In a speech at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">Rice</a> University in Houston, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-f-kennedy">John F. Kennedy</a> committed America to a Moon landing that decade, 'not because they are easy, but because they are hard'. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo-11">Apollo 11</a> landed seven years later.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-25">25 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Sonny Liston knocks out Floyd Patterson to win the world title</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Sonny Liston took the world heavyweight title in Chicago by knocking out Floyd Patterson in 2 minutes 6 seconds of the first round. He lost the title to Cassius Clay in 1964.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/london" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/london.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/october-5">5 October</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Dr. No premieres, launching the James Bond films</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Dr. No, starring Sean Connery as Ian Fleming's secret agent, premiered at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Pavilion. It began one of the longest-running series in cinema history.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/october-5">5 October</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The Beatles release their first single, 'Love Me Do'</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Parlophone released 'Love Me Do', the Beatles' debut single in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. It reached only number 17, but within a year the band from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> had become a national sensation.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/october-9">9 October</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Uganda gains independence from Britain</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Uganda became independent with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milton-obote">Milton Obote</a> as prime minister and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-of-buganda">Kabaka of Buganda</a> later as president. Its early decades were marked by political upheaval, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idi-amin">Idi Amin</a>.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/october-11">11 October</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Pope John XXIII opens the Second Vatican Council</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">More than 2,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> gathered in St Peter's Basilica for the opening of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-vatican-council">Second Vatican Council</a>. Over three years it introduced vernacular liturgy, embraced ecumenism and affirmed religious freedom.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/october-22">22 October</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Kennedy tells Americans of Soviet missiles in Cuba</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">In a televised address, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-f-kennedy">John F. Kennedy</a> revealed that Soviet nuclear missile sites were being built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and announced a naval 'quarantine' of the island. The crisis brought the superpowers closer to nuclear war than ever before.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/november-6">6 November</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">The UN General Assembly calls for sanctions on South Africa</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Resolution 1761 condemned apartheid and urged member states to break diplomatic ties and boycott South African trade. It also set up a special committee against apartheid.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/sino-indian-war" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sino-indian-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">War</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/sino-indian-war">Sino-Indian War</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Sino-Indian War was a border war fought between China and India in October and November 1962 over disputed Himalayan territory in Aksai Chin and the North-East Frontier Agency.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/evian-accords" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/evian-accords.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Treaty · Peace</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/evian-accords">Évian Accords</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Évian Accords were the agreements signed in 1962 between France and the Algerian National Liberation Front, ending the Algerian War.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-missile-crisis" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cuban-missile-crisis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Crisis</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/cuban-missile-crisis">Cuban Missile Crisis</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Cuban Missile Crisis of October 1962 was the closest the Cold War came to nuclear war.</p></div></li></ol></section>
